# Routh II.Art.156 — why rapid rotation steadies a body — dynamic notebook
#
# E. J. Routh, "The Advanced Part of a Treatise on the Dynamics of a System of
# Rigid Bodies", Part II (Macmillan, 6th ed. 1905), Art. 156: "It is well known
# that the steadiness or stability of a moving body is much increased by a
# rapid rotation about a principal axis. The reason of this is evident from
# what precedes."
#
# ROUTH'S REASON. It is the parallelogram of angular velocities. A disturbance
# adds a fixed transverse angular velocity to whatever the body already has.
# If the existing spin is n and the disturbance is d, the axis is tilted
# through an angle of order d/n. Double the spin and the SAME disturbance tilts
# the axis half as far. Nothing about the disturbance changes -- only its
# proportion to what is already there.
#
# THE MEASURABLE. For a uniaxal body (A = B, so the figure axis is C) the
# torque-free motion is a steady precession: the angle between the
# instantaneous axis and the invariable line is rigorously CONSTANT. That
# angle is the wobble, and it is what shrinks with n:
#
#     cos(angle) = (w . L) / (|w| |L|),     w = (d, 0, n),  L = (A d, 0, C n)
#
# THE BODY. A cuboid of mass 3 with half-extents (1, 1, 2) has
#
#     I = diag(5, 5, 2)      so   A = B = 5,   C = 2   (figure axis z)
#
# Three copies are spun about z at n = 1, 3 and 9, each given the SAME
# transverse disturbance d = 0.3. Predicted:
#
#     n = 1:  cos = 0.9386697595167284   angle = 20.171 deg   |L| = 2.5
#     n = 3:  cos = 0.9894610641341026   angle =  8.326 deg   |L| = 6.18465843842649
#     n = 9:  cos = 0.9987592287124399   angle =  2.854 deg   |L| = 18.062391868188442
#
# Nine times the spin, seven times less wobble, from an identical kick. Each
# cosine is constant for all time, which is the "steady" in steady precession:
# the axis sweeps a cone and never opens it further.
#
# SETUP NOTE. system.g_constant is set to 0 so the three bodies do not attract
# one another. Pairwise gravity is on by default with G = 1, and although
# gravity exerts no torque, letting the bodies fall together drives their
# separation towards the 1/r^2 singularity and destroys the integration -- a
# trap worth naming, because the rotational answer then looks wrong for a
# purely translational reason.
#
# WHAT YOU WATCH: three identical boxes given identical sideways kicks. The
# slow one lurches around a wide cone; the fast one barely nods.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# LAUNCH (run from the repository root: /Users/youruser/Developer/code/rust/rustSimulate)
#
#     posim_notebook routh_p2_spin_stabilisation
#
# or, without the shell alias, the always-available form:
#
#     cargo run -p posim --release -- --notebook dynamic_notebooks/routh_p2_spin_stabilisation.posim
#
# The scene window opens in your browser. To start the simulation, press Start
# in that window, or type this at the In[] prompt in the terminal:
#
#     scene start
#
# If no browser should be launched (the URL is printed instead), use:
#
#     POSIM_NO_BROWSER=1 cargo run -p posim --release -- --notebook dynamic_notebooks/routh_p2_spin_stabilisation.posim
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

set system.g_constant = 0
set system.rtol = 1e-13
set system.atol = 1e-15

new cuboid { mass = 3, half_extents = [1, 1, 2], position = [-6, 0, 0], angular_velocity = [0.3, 0, 1] }
new cuboid { mass = 3, half_extents = [1, 1, 2], position = [ 0, 0, 0], angular_velocity = [0.3, 0, 3] }
new cuboid { mass = 3, half_extents = [1, 1, 2], position = [ 6, 0, 0], angular_velocity = [0.3, 0, 9] }

get obj0.inertia_tensor

# the wobble angle of each, as cos(angle between omega and L), at t = 0
dot(obj0.angular_velocity, obj0.angular_momentum) / (norm(obj0.angular_velocity) * norm(obj0.angular_momentum))
dot(obj1.angular_velocity, obj1.angular_momentum) / (norm(obj1.angular_velocity) * norm(obj1.angular_momentum))
dot(obj2.angular_velocity, obj2.angular_momentum) / (norm(obj2.angular_velocity) * norm(obj2.angular_momentum))

run 10 steps 5

# ... and after ten units: identical. Steady precession never opens the cone.
dot(obj0.angular_velocity, obj0.angular_momentum) / (norm(obj0.angular_velocity) * norm(obj0.angular_momentum))
dot(obj1.angular_velocity, obj1.angular_momentum) / (norm(obj1.angular_velocity) * norm(obj1.angular_momentum))
dot(obj2.angular_velocity, obj2.angular_momentum) / (norm(obj2.angular_velocity) * norm(obj2.angular_momentum))
norm(obj0.angular_momentum)
norm(obj2.angular_momentum)
reset

set system.g_constant = 0
set system.rtol = 1e-13
set system.atol = 1e-15
new cuboid { mass = 3, half_extents = [1, 1, 2], position = [-6, 0, 0], angular_velocity = [0.3, 0, 1] }
new cuboid { mass = 3, half_extents = [1, 1, 2], position = [ 0, 0, 0], angular_velocity = [0.3, 0, 3] }
new cuboid { mass = 3, half_extents = [1, 1, 2], position = [ 6, 0, 0], angular_velocity = [0.3, 0, 9] }

scene create
scene set_time_step 0.02
